<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n13843" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Dĭdymē</span></b> ,
			ēs, f.
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>An island near Sicily</i>, <i><span class="en">Plin. 3, 9,
				14, § 94</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Mel. 2, 7,
				18</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Ov. F. 4, 475</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>An island in the Aegaean Sea</i>, <i><span class="en">Ov. M. 7,
				469</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>III.</b>
			<i>A city in Ionia</i> (also called <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Dĭdyma</span></b> ,
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Δίδυμα</span>), <i>in
				the Milesian territory, now Jeronda</i> or <i>Joran, with a temple and oracle of Apollo</i>, called <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Dĭdy-mēon</span></b> ,
			i, n., <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Διδυμεῖον</span>, <i><span class="en">Curt. 7, 5, 28</span></i>; cf. Mütz.
			and Zumpt, ad h. l.—Hence also, <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Dĭdymeus</span></b>  (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">trisyl.</span></b> ),
			ei, m., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Διδυμεύς</span>, <i>the Didymean</i>, i. e.
			Apollo, <i><span class="en">Mel. 1, 17, 1</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
